  {
    "id": 5131856461,
    "node_id": "PRR_kwDOALaj488AAAABMeHqTQ",
    "user": {
      "login": "annevk",
      "id": 1544111,
      "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjE1NDQxMTE=",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1544111?u=5d39bb7a31325e2905672ce29876c4c00e2a152e&v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/annevk",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/following{/other_user}",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/gists{/gist_id}",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/starred{/owner}{/repo}",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/annevk/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "body": "I don't understand something. Why are a lot of these methods only on `Element` and `ShadowRoot`? Why not have parity with the similarly-named DOM APIs?\r\n\r\nI guess the problem is that we don't have a context element then?\r\n\r\n<details><summary>More AI notes</summary>\r\n\r\n```\r\n  Remaining\r\n\r\n  Suggestions\r\n  \r\n  - source:126792-126800 — filter and set HTML is now the odd one out. It's the only one of the three helpers that self-maps via target for HTML insertion; the other two require the caller to pass a final parent. Worse, target now names two different\r\n  things in adjacent algorithms: the not-yet-mapped receiver in filter and set HTML, and the node being replaced in filter and replace with HTML. Either move the mapping out for symmetry (costs three extra Let target be … steps, in Element's\r\n  setHTML, setHTMLUnsafe, and innerHTML setter — exactly the shape appendHTML/prependHTML now have), or rename the parameter so a reader isn't led to think it's already resolved.\r\n  - source:126783-126788 — the new exception-type note is looser than the algorithm it annotates. \"modern positional insertion methods throw a HierarchyRequestError DOMException when tree hierarchy constraints are violated\" isn't what happens: they\r\n  throw for a specific parent set (Document, template element, hostless DocumentFragment). Generic constraint violations out of pre-insert/replace are unreachable here, since the parent is guaranteed to be an Element or hosted DocumentFragment and\r\n  the fragment is freshly created. Suggest ending it \"…the HTML insertion methods throw a \"HierarchyRequestError\" DOMException instead.\" Also, \"modern positional insertion methods\" and the preceding note's \"HTML insertion methods\" are two informal\r\n  names for the same undefined group — pick one.\r\n  \r\n  Nits\r\n\r\n  - source:126783 — data-x=\"dom-element-outerHTML\" (lowercase element) sits in the same sentence as data-x=\"dom-Element-insertAdjacentHTML\" (capital). Both resolve — the spec already has 13 lowercase dom-element- refs — but be internally consistent\r\n  in new text; the <dfn> is dom-Element-outerHTML.\r\n  - Commit body: \"ChildNode methods fail silently … and fails with a HierarchyRequestError\" (agreement); \"setting HTML to template elements\" → \"on\"; \"their template content\" → \"their template contents\" (the spec term); \"exposed also on\" → \"also\r\n  exposed on\".\r\n```\r\n</details>",
